    <h1>Random Video Call — Talk to Strangers Online in 2026</h1>

    <div class="blog-post-body">

      <h2>Introduction: What Is a Random Video Call?</h2>

      <p>A random video call is a real-time video conversation with someone you did not already know. Unlike messaging a friend or joining a scheduled meeting, random video chat is built around spontaneous encounters between people who may live in different cities, countries, or cultures.</p>

      <p>The idea is simple: two people enter a conversation, communicate through video and audio, and decide whether they want to continue talking. If the conversation is not a good fit, either person can leave and move on.</p>

      <p>Random video chat has changed considerably since the early days of anonymous chat websites. Modern users have more choices around communication format, privacy, moderation, reporting and device compatibility. However, the same basic rule still applies: meeting strangers online can be interesting and useful, but users should understand both the benefits and the risks.</p>

      <p>This guide explains how random video calling works, why people use it, how video communication compares with text and voice chat, what the available research tells us about online communication, and how to stay safe when talking with strangers online.</p>

      <h2>Video Calls Are Already Part of Everyday Communication</h2>

      <p>Video communication became much more common during the COVID-19 pandemic and has remained an established way for people to communicate remotely.</p>

      <p>Pew Research Center found that 81% of U.S. adults had used video calls at some point during the first year of the pandemic. Among adults under 30, roughly half had participated in virtual parties or social gatherings during that period.</p>

