      <h2>2. Why Do People Talk to Strangers Online?</h2>

      <p>Talking with someone you do not already know offers something different from communicating with an established friend. There is an element of uncertainty: you do not know exactly what the other person will be interested in, what they have experienced, or where the conversation will go.</p>

      <p>For some users, that spontaneity is the main attraction. For others, random conversations provide opportunities to practice a language, hear different perspectives, discuss hobbies, or simply have a short social interaction.</p>

      <h3>2.1 The Appeal of Spontaneous Conversation</h3>

      <p>Random conversations remove some of the expectations associated with established relationships. You may not need to maintain a long-term relationship or share an existing social circle.</p>

      <p>At the same time, spontaneity has a downside: not every conversation will be interesting, respectful or comfortable. The ability to leave quickly is therefore an important part of the experience.</p>

      <h3>2.2 Learning Without Traveling</h3>

      <p>Video conversations can provide a direct opportunity to hear how another person describes their culture, language, hobbies or everyday life.</p>

      <p>However, one person should never be treated as a representative of an entire country or culture. A stranger can provide a personal perspective, not a complete cultural education.</p>

      <h3>2.3 Social Interaction and Loneliness</h3>

      <p>Online communication can provide opportunities for social interaction, but it should not be described as a universal solution to loneliness. Research shows that loneliness is a complex experience and that people can feel lonely even when they have friends or communicate frequently.</p>

