      <p>For that reason, responsible online communication requires both technology and user judgment.</p>

      <h2>6. Privacy When Talking to Strangers Online</h2>

      <p>Privacy is particularly important in random video chat because users may communicate with people whose identities they do not know.</p>

      <p>Before using any online communication platform, read its privacy policy and understand what information is collected, how long it is retained, and whether third-party services are involved.</p>

      <h3>6.1 Do Not Confuse Anonymity With Complete Privacy</h3>

      <p>A service that does not require a public profile may collect less information directly from users, but this does not automatically mean that the service collects no technical information.</p>

      <p>Browser permissions, network connections, analytics systems, hosting infrastructure and other third-party technologies can all affect what information may be processed.</p>

      <h3>6.2 No Registration Does Not Mean No Risk</h3>

      <p>Not requiring an account can reduce the amount of profile information a user needs to provide. However, users should still avoid revealing sensitive information during a conversation.</p>

      <h3>6.3 Protect Your Personal Information</h3>

      <p>Never share passwords, one-time authentication codes, banking details, government identification numbers or your exact home address with a stranger.</p>

      <h2>7. What Can You Learn From Talking to Strangers?</h2>

      <p>A random conversation does not automatically become meaningful, but it can create opportunities to learn something you did not know before.</p>

      <h3>7.1 Language Practice</h3>

      <p>Speaking with someone who uses another language can provide exposure to pronunciation, vocabulary and everyday expressions.</p>

      <h3>7.2 Different Perspectives</h3>

      <p>People may approach the same topic differently because of their experiences, education, profession or culture. Asking respectful questions can reveal perspectives that you might not encounter in your normal social circle.</p>

      <h3>7.3 Conversation Skills</h3>

      <p>Random conversations can also be useful practice for communication skills. You can practice introducing yourself, asking open-ended questions, listening carefully and keeping a conversation going.</p>

      <h3>7.4 Knowing When to End a Conversation</h3>

      <p>An important social skill is recognizing when a conversation should end. If the interaction becomes uncomfortable, repetitive or inappropriate, ending the call is a reasonable choice.</p>

