      <h2>8. How to Stay Safe When Talking to Strangers</h2>

      <p>Random video chat can be enjoyable, but users should remember that the person on the other side of the camera is still a stranger.</p>

      <h3>8.1 Leave Conversations That Make You Uncomfortable</h3>

      <p>You do not need to justify leaving a conversation. If someone becomes aggressive, sexual, threatening or otherwise inappropriate, end the interaction and use the platform's reporting or blocking tools when available.</p>

      <h3>8.2 Do Not Send Money</h3>

      <p>Be particularly cautious if someone you have just met asks for money, financial assistance, gift cards, cryptocurrency or investment opportunities.</p>

      <p>The U.S. Federal Trade Commission warns that online scammers may build relationships with people before eventually asking for money. The same basic warning applies when meeting strangers through online communication services.</p>

      <h3>8.3 Be Careful About Moving Conversations Elsewhere</h3>

      <p>A stranger may ask you to move the conversation to another platform. Moving elsewhere can make it harder to use the original service's reporting or moderation tools, so consider whether there is a genuine reason to do so.</p>

      <h3>8.4 Remember That People Can Misrepresent Themselves</h3>

      <p>A username, profile picture, claimed location or personal story is not proof of identity. Avoid making important decisions based solely on what an online stranger tells you.</p>

